1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,966
Welcome to lectures seven and eight:

2
00:00:02,966 --> 00:00:09,062
numerical and categorical variables, non-graphical versus graphical E D A.

3
00:00:09,062 --> 00:00:17,136
We apply every technique to every feature of one large real dataset, the Superstore retail orders.

4
00:00:17,936 --> 00:00:31,000
Our dataset has eight thousand three hundred ninety nine real retail orders for office supplies, technology and furniture, with full sales, discount and shipping detail.

5
00:00:31,000 --> 00:00:39,580
Everyone understands buying something online, and unlike Titanic or Iris, most students have never analysed it.

6
00:00:39,580 --> 00:00:48,624
We separate non-graphical, statistical E D A from graphical, visual E D A, apply both to every feature, then compare.

7
00:00:48,624 --> 00:00:55,040
Numbers first for precision, plots second for shape, then check that the two agree.

8
00:00:55,840 --> 00:00:58,636
There are six numerical features.

9
00:00:58,636 --> 00:01:01,941
Sales is the dollar value of the order.

10
00:01:01,941 --> 00:01:07,025
Profit is the dollar profit or loss, and it can be negative.

11
00:01:07,025 --> 00:01:12,194
Discount is the rate, from zero to about twenty five percent.

12
00:01:12,194 --> 00:01:15,922
Order quantity runs from one to fifty units.

13
00:01:15,922 --> 00:01:20,752
Unit price is the price of a single unit before discount.

14
00:01:20,752 --> 00:01:24,904
And shipping cost is the dollar cost of shipping.

15
00:01:25,704 --> 00:01:28,458
And three categorical features.

16
00:01:28,458 --> 00:01:29,968
Product category:

17
00:01:29,968 --> 00:01:33,610
office supplies, technology or furniture.

18
00:01:33,610 --> 00:01:34,498
Ship mode:

19
00:01:34,498 --> 00:01:38,318
regular air, delivery truck or express air.

20
00:01:38,318 --> 00:01:40,184
And customer segment:

21
00:01:40,184 --> 00:01:44,714
consumer, corporate, home office or small business.

22
00:01:44,714 --> 00:01:51,288
Together they describe what was bought, how it shipped, and who bought it.

23
00:01:52,088 --> 00:02:01,350
The very first non-graphical check on any dataset is counting empty cells, before any statistic or plot.

24
00:02:01,350 --> 00:02:05,091
One line does it for every column at once:

25
00:02:05,091 --> 00:02:06,872
df dot isna dot sum.

26
00:02:07,672 --> 00:02:16,932
Product base margin is missing for sixty three orders, just zero point seven five percent, a small and easily flagged gap.

27
00:02:16,932 --> 00:02:26,344
Every one of the six numerical features we use today has zero missing values, and the categorical features are complete too.

28
00:02:27,144 --> 00:02:30,124
Technique one is central tendency.

29
00:02:30,124 --> 00:02:35,732
The mean is the arithmetic average and is sensitive to extremes.

30
00:02:35,732 --> 00:02:40,289
The median is the middle value and resists extremes.

31
00:02:40,289 --> 00:02:43,444
The mode is the most frequent value.

32
00:02:43,444 --> 00:02:47,213
Each is one line for all numerical columns:

33
00:02:47,213 --> 00:02:50,280
mean, median, mode, no loop needed.

34
00:02:51,080 --> 00:02:54,905
Profit shows the most dramatic gap of any feature:

35
00:02:54,905 --> 00:03:00,947
the mean is one hundred eighty one dollars while the median is minus one fifty.

36
00:03:00,947 --> 00:03:05,231
The average and the typical order tell opposite stories.

37
00:03:05,231 --> 00:03:10,126
Order quantity and discount have mean and median close together.

38
00:03:10,126 --> 00:03:17,240
And sales and unit price have a mean far above the median, a first hint of strong right skew.

39
00:03:18,040 --> 00:03:20,403
Technique two is dispersion.

40
00:03:20,403 --> 00:03:25,045
Standard deviation is the typical spread from the mean.

41
00:03:25,045 --> 00:03:30,868
Range is maximum minus minimum and is sensitive to one extreme value.

42
00:03:30,868 --> 00:03:38,886
The I Q R is Q three minus Q one, the spread of the middle fifty percent, and resists extremes.

43
00:03:38,886 --> 00:03:43,696
Again one line computes each statistic for every feature.

44
00:03:44,496 --> 00:03:52,857
Sales has a standard deviation of thirty five eighty five, more than twice its I Q R of fifteen sixty six.

45
00:03:52,857 --> 00:03:57,353
A handful of very large orders are stretching the spread.

46
00:03:57,353 --> 00:04:04,925
Profit's standard deviation is over four times its I Q R, the same pattern for gains and losses.

47
00:04:04,925 --> 00:04:10,683
Order quantity's standard deviation and I Q R are in sensible proportion:

48
00:04:10,683 --> 00:04:12,576
well-behaved dispersion.

49
00:04:13,376 --> 00:04:15,373
Technique three is shape.

50
00:04:15,373 --> 00:04:24,319
Skewness of zero means symmetric, positive means a long right tail, and the further from zero the more lopsided.

51
00:04:24,319 --> 00:04:27,674
Kurtosis measures how heavy the tails are:

52
00:04:27,674 --> 00:04:35,343
very high kurtosis means extreme values are far more common than a normal distribution predicts.

53
00:04:35,343 --> 00:04:39,656
Skew and kurtosis are one line each for every feature.

54
00:04:40,456 --> 00:04:50,818
Unit price is extraordinarily skewed, fourteen point one three, with kurtosis of two seventy one, the most extreme feature.

55
00:04:50,818 --> 00:04:58,990
Sales, skew five point four, and profit, skew three point six five, are also dramatically skewed.

56
00:04:58,990 --> 00:05:05,392
Order quantity and discount sit almost exactly at zero, genuinely symmetric.

57
00:05:06,192 --> 00:05:15,284
Ranked by skewness, unit price towers over every other feature, so extreme that it barely fits on the same chart.

58
00:05:15,284 --> 00:05:18,502
Order quantity and discount sit at zero.

59
00:05:18,502 --> 00:05:28,076
The numbers tell us sales, profit and unit price are wildly skewed, but they cannot show us what that shape looks like.

60
00:05:28,076 --> 00:05:30,168
For that we need pictures.

